    "q-347130": {
      "dom_id": "step-q347130",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Write the following series in sigma notation in terms of index $n.$\n\n$5-\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{5}{9}+…={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The first term is ${a}_{1}=5$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Using these two terms, we compute the common ratio:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{3})}{5}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general term can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{(r)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{-1}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & =5(-3){(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & =(-15){(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the infinite geometric series can be written in sigma notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{5}{9}+⋯ & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,(-15){(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
          "choices": [],
