    "q-350557": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350557",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the sequence starting from ${a}_{1}=9$  with the property $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$ Write the sum to infinity $S$ of the terms of this series using sigma notation in terms of index $n.$\n\n$S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the sequence is a geometric sequence since the ratio of any two successive terms is constant.\n\nThe first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=9,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general term ${a}_{n}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{(r)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =9{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =9{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{-1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & =27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the infinite geometric series $S$ can be written in sigma notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
